Hi

Hear are two patches that I have been using for a long time. I have finally 
finished them and will grant them to the community. These are only useful for 
*nix users, so m$-fans may click their mouses on the Next-button. These work 
in vanilla and in Slash'EM. The patches are:
- Message Pipe: Allow sending NetHAck top-line messages to an external program 
  for further handling.
- VT100 arrow keys: Modifies the Unix TTY interface so that arrow keys may be 
  used, if the terminal [emulator] is VT100-like.
  
Details:
MESSAGE PIPE

Message pipe creates a Unix pipe between NetHAck and an external program. This 
external program may do whatever it wants to with these messages. This is very 
useful for example in creating a windowing system independent message -> sound 
effect hooker. When it comes to gathering information about played games, the 
possibilities are infinite. Also players without (or with low) vision might
want to attach NetHack to a speech synthesiser to speed up playing.

The patch contains a README which has more details.

VT100 ARROW KEYS

This patch allows using arrow keys under Unix in the TTY interface for 
movement, like in the DOS version. Also it fixes the problem that 
ALT-shortcuts don't work when ALT generates an escape prefix rather than 
setting the 8th bit, which is the case at least in 8-bit aware Linux systems. 
The patch file contains more information.

These patches are available from http://thack.org/nethack/. For Message
Pipe there are separate vanilla and Slash'EM versions (cosmetic difference 
only), but the VT100 patch applies cleanly to both source trees.

I have asked the Great DevTeam about these both, but all I've got is:
"No arrow keys, since X UI has different use for them." I don't understand 
what does X have to do with TTY, but I don't understand DevTeam very well in 
general. But what comes to the Slash'EM developers, I wish they could 
integrate Message Pipe into their great game, since it would greatly help
blind players (NetHack is proud of its accessibility, right?) and other people
could also find it interesting.

